<rml>
<head>
	<title>System Menu</title>
	<link type="text/rcss" href="base.rcss"/>
	<link type="text/rcss" href="sys_menu.rcss"/>
</head>
<body>
	<!-- Modern theme's own copy of the system menu -- per-theme file (UI::RmlBridge::
	     LoadThemedDocument) because modern-frame is genuinely modern-specific structure, not just
	     style -- the shared ../../sys_menu.rml is legacy's own theme-neutral copy and carries none
	     of it (a "shared" file must not bake in one theme's vocabulary, same reasoning login.rml/
	     msg_win.rml/remember_password_prompt.rml/my_inventory.rml/my_quest_info.rml already forked
	     for). .panel-cap-top/.panel-middle/.panel-cap-bottom stay in the markup (SysMenuWin.cpp
	     binds nothing to them directly) but render as nothing under modern -- this theme's own
	     base.rcss/sys_menu.rcss declare no matching rule for them, unlike legacy's real
	     sprite-backed versions. Keep this file's ids/data-model/event-callback names in sync with
	     ../../sys_menu.rml -- SysMenuWin.cpp binds against both by the same names.

	     Uses base.rcss's shared forged-dialog shell (also used by login.rml), but with the metal
	     .modern-header-rail header and a single center .modern-joint rivet, rather than Login's
	     crimson .modern-title-plate hero-banner -- the two are alternative header styles for
	     different windows, never combined (see login.rml's own note on this). No -crimson frame
	     variant either: crimson belongs to a hero-banner's own plate, and this dialog has no
	     hero-banner. -->
	<div id="backdrop"></div>
	<div id="panel" class="center-both modern-frame" data-class-compact="select_server_hidden" data-model="sys_menu">
		<div id="panel_middle" class="panel-middle"></div>
		<!-- Exterior blackened-metal silhouette (outer iron edge, -3dp) + inner groove (~4dp
		     inward) -- same shell as Login's #panel, just applied to a plain (non-crimson) frame. -->
		<div class="modern-shell-edge"></div>
		<div class="modern-groove"></div>

		<!-- Raised iron header rail with its own single center-rivet stud, plus the title text.
		     No separate "System" eyebrow -- redundant with "System Menu" already saying it. -->
		<div class="modern-header-rail">
			<div class="modern-joint"></div>
			<div class="modern-header-accent"></div>
			<div class="modern-header-text">
				<div class="modern-header-title">{{system_menu_label}}</div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="panel-cap-top"></div>
		<div class="panel-cap-bottom"></div>
		<div id="btn_exit_game" class="btn" data-event-click="sysmenu_exit_game_click">{{exit_game_label}}</div>
		<div id="btn_select_server" class="btn" data-class-hidden="select_server_hidden" data-event-click="sysmenu_select_server_click">{{select_server_label}}</div>
		<div id="btn_option" class="btn" data-event-click="sysmenu_option_click">{{option_label}}</div>
		<div id="btn_close" class="btn" data-event-click="sysmenu_close_click">{{close_label}}</div>
	</div>
</body>
</rml>
